|}

The web dashboard always listens on port '''4000'''.

=== Runtime configuration ===

Most settings are managed at runtime through the dashboard (and the REST API) and are persisted in SQLite:

* '''Listen port''' — change the charge‑point WebSocket port; the listener restarts.
* '''Backend servers''' — add, edit, enable/disable, or delete up to '''3''' upstream CSMS targets.
* '''Command blocking rules''' — global (<code>*</code>) or per charge point.
* '''Boot overrides''' — per charge point field rewrites.
* '''Equipment database''' — vendors, models, and meter types used by the override UI.

=== Database ===

SQLite database at <code>{PROXY_DATA_DIR}/ocppproxy.db</code> (WAL mode), created automatically.

{| class="wikitable"
! Table !! Purpose
|-
| <code>backend_servers</code> || Upstream CSMS targets (max 3); seeded with <code>ws://127.0.0.1:9000/ocpp</code>
|-
| <code>blocked_commands</code> || Command blocking rules (<code>command</code>, <code>cp_id</code> or <code>*</code>, reason)
|-
| <code>boot_overrides</code> || Per‑CP BootNotification field overrides
|-
| <code>connector_status</code> || Desired connector availability overrides (connector 0 = whole station)
|-
| <code>command_stats</code> || Aggregate counts per command / direction / charge point
|-
| <code>message_log</code> || Detailed message history (Heartbeat excluded)
|-
| <code>event_log</code> || Paired request/response events
|-
| <code>equipment_vendors</code>, <code>equipment_models</code>, <code>meter_types</code> || Lookup data for the override UI (pre‑populated)
|-
| <code>proxy_config</code> || Key‑value settings (e.g. <code>listen_port</code>)
|}

== Using the Web Dashboard ==

Open the dashboard at <code>http://&lt;proxy-host&gt;:4000/</code>. It uses Socket.IO (namespace <code>/proxy</code>) for live updates. The dashboard is organised into the following sections.

=== Events (Live) ===

A real‑time stream of OCPP messages (Heartbeat excluded). Each row shows the timestamp, charge point, direction, action, and a status badge (OK, ERROR, BLOCKED, PENDING). Requests are paired with their responses (30‑second timeout for orphaned calls). Click a row to view the full JSON payload. Filters by charge point, direction, and action are available, along with an auto‑scroll toggle. The live view keeps up to 500 rows.

=== Event Log ===

Persistent paired request/response history, searchable by content and severity and filterable by OCPP command. Each entry records timestamp, charge point, direction, backend, command, and message, with a modal showing formatted payloads.

=== Statistics ===

Command usage analytics: total messages, blocked count, unique actions, and per command/direction counts with last‑seen timestamps.

=== Command Blocking ===

Create rules to reject specific OCPP commands. Choose the command, the scope (all charge points via <code>*</code>, or a specific charge point ID), and an optional reason. Blocked calls receive a <code>CallError</code> response and are logged with a blocked flag.

=== Boot Overrides ===

Rewrite BootNotification fields per charge point: vendor, model, serial number, firmware version, and meter type. Use the cascading vendor → model selector from the equipment database and toggle each override on or off. Overridden fields replace the original values before the message is forwarded to the CSMS.

=== Connector Control ===

Send a <code>ChangeAvailability</code> command directly to a connected charge point. Select the station and connector (0 = whole station, 1–N = individual connector) and a target status (Available, Unavailable, etc.). The proxy injects the command and consumes the charge point's response without forwarding it to the backend.

=== Proxy Settings ===

* '''WebSocket listen port''' — change the charge‑point port (1024–65535); the listener restarts.
* '''Backend central systems''' — manage up to 3 upstream servers (name, URL, enabled). Changing backends reconnects all charge points.

=== Equipment Database ===

Manage the vendor, model, and meter‑type lookups used by the Boot Overrides UI (add, rename, delete).

