== How Proxying Works ==

=== Message forwarding ===

OCPP messages use the JSON array framing:

<pre>
CALL        [2, "<unique_id>", "Action", {payload}]
CALL_RESULT [3, "<unique_id>", {response}]
CALL_ERROR  [4, "<unique_id>", "<error_code>", "<description>", {}]
</pre>

* '''Charge point → CSMS''': the proxy reads each message, checks blocking rules, applies BootNotification and connector‑status overrides, then forwards to all enabled backends. The message is logged and emitted to the dashboard.
* '''CSMS → charge point''': the proxy reads each message, checks blocking rules, forwards it to the charge point, and logs/emits it.

=== Multiple backends & failover ===

Each charge point message is fanned out to every enabled backend (one connection and queue per backend). If a backend is unreachable, the proxy buffers messages and retries; on reconnect it replays buffered messages and resumes normal forwarding, emitting <code>backend_offline</code> / <code>backend_reconnected</code> events to the dashboard.

=== Blocking and modification ===

* '''Blocking''': a matching rule causes the proxy to return a <code>CallError</code> to the sender instead of forwarding; the event is counted and logged.
* '''Boot overrides''': BootNotification fields are rewritten on the fly before forwarding.
* '''Status overrides''': reported connector status is replaced per charge point (connector 0 applies to all connectors).
* '''Injected commands''': commands sent from the dashboard are tracked so the charge point's response is consumed by the proxy and not forwarded upstream.

=== Known OCPP 1.6 actions ===

<code>Authorize</code>, <code>BootNotification</code>, <code>ChangeAvailability</code>, <code>ChangeConfiguration</code>, <code>ClearCache</code>, <code>DataTransfer</code>, <code>DiagnosticsStatusNotification</code>, <code>FirmwareStatusNotification</code>, <code>GetConfiguration</code>, <code>Heartbeat</code>, <code>MeterValues</code>, <code>RemoteStartTransaction</code>, <code>RemoteStopTransaction</code>, <code>Reset</code>, <code>StartTransaction</code>, <code>StatusNotification</code>, <code>StopTransaction</code>, <code>TriggerMessage</code>, <code>UnlockConnector</code>.

== Troubleshooting ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Symptom !! Things to check
|-
| Charge points cannot connect || Confirm chargers point to <code>ws://&lt;proxy&gt;:9100/ocpp/&lt;cp_id&gt;</code>; check the listen port in Proxy Settings and firewall rules.
|-
| Messages not reaching the CSMS || Verify at least one backend is enabled and its URL is reachable; watch for <code>backend_offline</code> events in the dashboard.
|-
| Dashboard not loading || Ensure the service is running and port 4000 is open; check the gunicorn error log.
|-
| A command is unexpectedly rejected || Review active rules in '''Command Blocking'''; look for the BLOCKED badge in the live events.
|-
| BootNotification shows wrong details || Check '''Boot Overrides''' for an enabled override on that charge point.
|-
| Multiple listeners / port conflicts || Run gunicorn with a single worker (<code>--workers 1</code>).
|}

== Quick Deployment Checklist ==

# Install Python 3 and run <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code> in a virtual environment.
# Create <code>/opt/charger/OCPPProxy/</code>, <code>/opt/charger/data/</code>, and <code>/var/log/charger/</code>.
# Install and enable the systemd service (single gunicorn worker).
# Open the dashboard at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:4000/</code>.
# In '''Proxy Settings''', confirm the listen port (9100) and configure the backend CSMS URL(s).
# Point charge points at <code>ws://&lt;proxy&gt;:9100/ocpp/&lt;cp_id&gt;</code>.
# (Optional) Configure command blocking, boot overrides, and connector control as needed.
